Below is a short “data‑discovery cheat‑sheet” that shows  
• which columns will probably matter for the requested analysis,  
• a set of 10 incrementally more complex Snowflake SQL snippets (each limited to 20 rows) that you can run to familiarise yourself with the data before building the final query.

──────────────────────────────────
POTENTIALLY RELEVANT COLUMNS
──────────────────────────────────
"damage_property":   used to rank the 100 costliest events (in USD)  
"event_begin_time":  numeric timestamp of event start – extract year / month to isolate the last 15 years and group by month  
"event_id":          unique identifier of every single storm event (needed when de‑duplicating)  
"state", "event_type": optional descriptive fields that may help you verify your result set  

Typical time/season filters you may use later  
• WHERE TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ("event_begin_time"/1e6) >= DATEADD(year,-15,CURRENT_DATE())  
• MONTH(TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ("event_begin_time"/1e6))  → month number (1–12)  

